From f3fc5f82427d027ebbeb44d7fa244efe895110bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Lothian <mike@fireburn.co.uk>
Date: Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:20:52 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 104/108] drm/vino: add the encrypted control protocol

Add typed control-message encoding, AKE session handling, HDCP 2.2
transcript processing, and DisplayLink DL3 protocol constants.

Reuse DRM HDCP message identifiers and field lengths together with the
kernel AES, CMAC, SHA-256, HMAC, RSA akcipher, and random interfaces. Keep
session keys in memory-wiping Secret values and reuse the configured
receiver RSA transform for each downstream authentication.

Every message the dock accepts is built here, sealed with AES-CTR and the
vendor's CMAC construction over a distinct nonce, and every reply is
authenticated before a single field of it is read: the tag is what
identifies which of the inbound nonces a frame used, so decoding cannot be
talked into accepting a frame by its shape. Counters, reply classes and
per-head authentication state are validated before encrypted setup can
complete.

The two dock generations share this protocol but not all of its framing. A
per-head record names its connector as a one-based head number on DL-6xxx
and as a one-hot bit on DL-7000; a mode set carries its pixel clock as a
32-bit field that only DL-7000 fills above 16 bits; and a presence probe
reports an attached sink in its status word rather than by which handler
answered it. Those are stated once each, in the builder that needs them.

Keep DisplayLink-specific framing and key derivation local to the driver.

